
# crash dumps from shell tooling — never publishable
*.stackdump

# Internal git hooks -- never cross the vault/public boundary.
# Added as a purely defensive guard, per a review finding:
# .githooks/pre-push was sitting here untracked AND un-ignored, so any
# `git add -A` would have swept it into the public repo. Measured contents:
# an internal control name x3, the private vault's directory name x7,
# internal schema vocabulary x15, and the maintainer's real first name x3 --
# the last being the very thing the history purge exists to remove.
# This entry closes the accidental-sweep path only. The separate question of
# whether those files should ever be tracked deliberately is NOT settled by
# this line and is held for the publication-boundary review.
.githooks/

# Node dev tooling -- never publishable.
# Added BEFORE the first `npm install` in this repo, because
# until this line existed an install would have staged node_modules/ --
# and, if the browser cache ever landed in-tree, a Chromium binary --
# into the public repo. The site itself is backend-free and ships no
# JavaScript dependencies: everything under node_modules/ is developer
# tooling (see package.json) and none of it is served to a reader.
node_modules/

# Playwright's browser binaries live in the user cache
# (~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright on macOS), NOT in this tree. These two
# entries are defensive only, for the case where someone sets
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=0 and the download lands beside the code.
.cache/ms-playwright/
ms-playwright/
